-- AWS SCS Domain 1 (Incident Response) expansion: adds flashcards sc-f-200..227/sc-a-100..108/sc-y-050..054 and questions sc-q-200..233 for objectives 1.1 (IR plan design: Incident Manager response plans/contacts/escalation/runbooks vs playbooks, chat channel integration, replication sets, break-glass access, pre-provisioned forensic roles, tabletop exercises, Correction of Error post-incident review, per-scenario runbooks, AWS CIRT/abuse escalation), 1.2 (detect: GuardDuty specialized protection plans, delegated administrator, trusted IP/threat lists, Security Hub delegated administrator/cross-Region aggregation/severity+workflow status, Inspector risk score/reachability), and 1.3 (respond: STS session revocation via explicit deny + aws:TokenIssueTime, GuardDuty InstanceCredentialExfiltration finding, Security Hub custom actions vs automatic EventBridge remediation, isolate-vs-terminate, quarantine security group scope, Lambda containment, login-profile/MFA revocation, forensic snapshot chain of custody, SSM Automation runbooks as remediation targets).

insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
-- 1.1 IR plan design: core
('sc-f-200','awsscs','core',1,'1.1',$$What are the four phases of the incident response lifecycle AWS recommends for a security incident response plan?$$,$$Preparation (tooling, roles, access, runbooks in place before anything happens), Detection & Analysis (identify and triage), Containment/Eradication/Recovery (limit damage, remove the cause, restore normal operation), and Post-Incident Activity (root-cause review and improving the plan) - the same NIST SP 800-61 cycle AWS's own incident response guidance follows.$$),
('sc-f-201','awsscs','core',1,'1.1',$$What does an SSM Incident Manager response plan actually define?$$,$$The reusable link between a trigger (a CloudWatch alarm or manual start) and the response: which engagement plan/contacts to page, which chat channel to open, and which runbook (SSM Automation document) to run - so responders don't improvise the process during a live incident.$$),
('sc-f-202','awsscs','core',1,'1.1',$$What is a contact's escalation plan in Incident Manager?$$,$$An ordered set of stages, each with a wait time and one or more notification channels (SMS, voice call, email, chat), that automatically moves to the next stage - and can page a different person - if the current stage doesn't get acknowledged in time.$$),
('sc-f-203','awsscs','core',1,'1.1',$$How does a runbook differ from a playbook in incident response?$$,$$A runbook is a specific, executable set of steps (often an SSM Automation document) for one well-defined action, like isolating an instance. A playbook is the broader decision-making guide for a whole incident type - which runbooks to invoke, who to involve, and in what order - covering branching scenarios a single runbook doesn't.$$),
('sc-f-204','awsscs','core',1,'1.1',$$How does Incident Manager integrate with chat tools during an incident?$$,$$Through AWS Chatbot, it can automatically create a dedicated Slack or Amazon Chime channel when an incident starts, pulling in engaged responders so collaboration and the incident timeline happen in one place instead of scattered channels.$$),
('sc-f-205','awsscs','core',1,'1.1',$$What is an Incident Manager replication set, and why does it matter?$$,$$The set of AWS Regions Incident Manager data (response plans, contacts, incident records) is replicated across. Configuring at least two Regions means the service - and your ability to manage an incident - keeps working even if the primary Region has a problem.$$),
('sc-f-206','awsscs','core',1,'1.1',$$What is a break-glass access pattern in incident response planning?$$,$$A pre-provisioned, tightly controlled emergency IAM role or account that responders can assume quickly when normal access is unavailable or too slow (e.g., an SSO outage) - designed and tested in advance, with strong logging, rather than improvised during the incident.$$),
('sc-f-207','awsscs','core',1,'1.1',$$Why should a forensic/incident-response IAM role be created before an incident, not during one?$$,$$Creating least-privilege access under pressure is slow and error-prone, and it delays containment while an attacker has more time active. Pre-provisioning a scoped, tested, break-glass-style role means responders can act immediately when a real incident starts.$$),
('sc-f-208','awsscs','core',1,'1.1',$$What is a tabletop exercise (game day) in the context of an incident response plan?$$,$$A scheduled, simulated walk-through of a specific incident scenario (e.g., a leaked key, ransomware) where the team practices the response plan and runbooks without a real incident occurring - it surfaces gaps and stale steps before they matter.$$),
('sc-f-209','awsscs','core',1,'1.1',$$What should a post-incident review (AWS calls this a Correction of Error, or COE) produce?$$,$$A documented root cause, a timeline of what happened and what was done, and concrete corrective actions - including updates to the runbooks and response plan themselves, so the same gap doesn't recur next time.$$),
('sc-f-210','awsscs','core',1,'1.1',$$Why should an incident response plan include separate runbooks per incident type rather than one generic runbook?$$,$$Containing a leaked credential, a ransomware infection, and a DDoS attack call for different first actions and different stakeholders. Scenario-specific runbooks let responders execute the right steps immediately instead of adapting a one-size-fits-all checklist under pressure.$$),
('sc-f-211','awsscs','core',1,'1.1',$$When should you engage AWS's own incident response resources during a security event?$$,$$For suspected abuse of AWS resources (e.g., your account being used to attack others) or when you need AWS-side help investigating account compromise, escalate to AWS Support/the AWS Trust & Safety team - your IR plan should document this escalation path and who is authorized to invoke it in advance.$$),
-- 1.2 detect: core
('sc-f-212','awsscs','core',1,'1.2',$$Beyond core threat detection, what specialized GuardDuty protection plans can you enable?$$,$$S3 Protection (monitors S3 data-plane events for suspicious access), EKS Protection (analyzes Kubernetes audit logs), RDS Protection (profiles login activity for anomalies), Lambda Protection (analyzes network activity from function invocations), and Malware Protection (agentless EBS/S3 object scanning) - each adds a data source GuardDuty's ML models analyze.$$),
('sc-f-213','awsscs','core',1,'1.2',$$How do you get one account's-worth view of GuardDuty findings across an entire AWS Organization?$$,$$Designate a GuardDuty delegated administrator account. GuardDuty is then automatically enabled (or centrally managed) for existing and new member accounts, and their findings roll up to the delegated administrator without each account configuring GuardDuty separately.$$),
('sc-f-214','awsscs','core',1,'1.2',$$What is a GuardDuty trusted IP list, and how does it differ from a threat list?$$,$$A trusted IP list is a set of known-safe IPs (e.g., your own scanners) that GuardDuty won't generate findings for, reducing noise. A threat list is the opposite - custom known-bad IPs you want flagged even if GuardDuty's own intel wouldn't catch them - both configured by the delegated administrator.$$),
('sc-f-215','awsscs','core',1,'1.2',$$What does designating a Security Hub delegated administrator enable across an Organization?$$,$$One account can automatically enable Security Hub for existing and new member accounts and aggregate their findings; combined with cross-Region aggregation, that account gets a single view of findings from every enabled Region and account instead of checking each one separately.$$),
('sc-f-216','awsscs','core',1,'1.2',$$What do a Security Hub finding's severity and workflow status fields help an analyst do?$$,$$Severity (informational through critical) ranks how urgent a finding is; workflow status (NEW, NOTIFIED, RESOLVED, SUPPRESSED) tracks where it is in triage. Together they let a team filter to what needs attention now and avoid re-reviewing findings already handled or accepted.$$),
('sc-f-217','awsscs','core',1,'1.2',$$What does Amazon Inspector's risk score add beyond just listing a CVE?$$,$$It factors in the CVE's severity plus context like network reachability (is the vulnerable resource actually exposed to the internet?) and exploitability, so teams can prioritize the handful of findings that are both severe and actually reachable instead of triaging every CVE equally.$$),
-- 1.3 respond: core
('sc-f-218','awsscs','core',1,'1.3',$$If you only delete a compromised IAM user's access key, why might the compromise not actually be contained?$$,$$Any temporary credentials already issued via STS (assumed roles, federated sessions) remain valid until they expire on their own - deleting the long-term key doesn't revoke them. Full containment needs an explicit deny or session-revocation policy, not just key deletion.$$),
('sc-f-219','awsscs','core',1,'1.3',$$How do you immediately invalidate a principal's already-issued temporary STS credentials, not just future ones?$$,$$Attach a policy with an explicit Deny on all actions, conditioned on aws:TokenIssueTime before the moment of compromise. Because IAM evaluates policy on every API call, that Deny takes effect instantly and blocks even sessions already in progress - re-authentication after the fix issues a new token that's exempt.$$),
('sc-f-220','awsscs','core',1,'1.3',$$What does the GuardDuty finding type UnauthorizedAccess:IAMUser/InstanceCredentialExfiltration.OutsideAWS indicate?$$,$$An EC2 instance's role credentials (normally only used from within AWS) were used to make an API call from an IP address outside AWS - a strong signal those instance-role credentials were stolen (e.g., via SSRF) and are being used remotely by an attacker.$$),
('sc-f-221','awsscs','core',1,'1.3',$$What is a Security Hub custom action used for in automated response?$$,$$It lets an analyst manually select one or more findings in the console and send them to a specific EventBridge rule, which then triggers a Lambda function (or Step Functions workflow) to remediate - a human-in-the-loop trigger point, distinct from a fully automatic EventBridge rule matching every finding of a type.$$),
('sc-f-222','awsscs','core',1,'1.3',$$Why isolate a compromised EC2 instance instead of terminating it right away?$$,$$Terminating destroys volatile evidence (running processes, memory, network connections) needed to determine root cause and scope. Isolating with a quarantine security group stops further damage and lateral movement while keeping the instance available for snapshotting and memory capture.$$),
('sc-f-223','awsscs','core',1,'1.3',$$What should a quarantine security group used for containment actually allow?$$,$$As close to nothing as possible - deny all inbound and outbound by default - with, at most, a narrow exception for traffic to/from a designated forensic analysis host. It should not simply be a stricter version of the instance's normal rules; the goal is to fully cut off attacker access while preserving the instance for investigation.$$),
('sc-f-224','awsscs','core',1,'1.3',$$How do you contain a compromised Lambda function?$$,$$Remove or disable its event source mappings and triggers so it stops being invoked, and revoke or scope down its execution role's permissions (e.g., attach a deny policy) so even a manual invocation can't do further damage - there's no EC2-style security group to isolate a function with.$$),
('sc-f-225','awsscs','core',1,'1.3',$$Besides revoking sessions, what should happen to a compromised IAM user's sign-in ability?$$,$$Disable or delete the console login profile (and any MFA devices tied to it) in addition to deactivating access keys, so the attacker can't simply switch to console sign-in - and rotate/replace any secrets (e.g., in Secrets Manager) the user's credentials could have exposed.$$),
('sc-f-226','awsscs','core',1,'1.3',$$Why copy a forensic EBS snapshot into a separate, locked-down account before analyzing it?$$,$$It preserves a clean, immutable chain of custody: analysis happens away from the still-compromised account (and any attacker who might still have access there), and the original evidence in the workload account stays untouched in case it's needed again or for legal purposes.$$),
('sc-f-227','awsscs','core',1,'1.3',$$What role do SSM Automation documents play in automated incident remediation?$$,$$They're the executable runbook a Security Hub custom action or EventBridge rule invokes - encoding steps like isolating an instance, snapshotting a volume, or revoking credentials as a repeatable, auditable automation instead of a human running ad hoc commands during a live incident.$$),
-- acronyms
('sc-a-100','awsscs','acronym',1,'1.1',$$COE$$,$$Correction of Error - AWS's term for a structured post-incident review documenting root cause, timeline, and corrective actions.$$),
('sc-a-101','awsscs','acronym',1,'1.1',$$CIRT$$,$$AWS Customer Incident Response Team - AWS's own security team that customers can engage for help investigating account compromise or reporting resource abuse.$$),
('sc-a-102','awsscs','acronym',1,'1.1',$$TTX$$,$$Tabletop Exercise - a simulated incident walk-through used to test and refine a response plan and runbooks without a real incident.$$),
('sc-a-103','awsscs','acronym',1,'1.2',$$CVE$$,$$Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures - the public identifier scheme Amazon Inspector uses to report known vulnerabilities it finds.$$),
('sc-a-104','awsscs','acronym',1,'1.2',$$CVSS$$,$$Common Vulnerability Scoring System - the standardized severity score behind a vulnerability, which Inspector combines with reachability to compute its risk score.$$),
('sc-a-105','awsscs','acronym',1,'1.2',$$IOC$$,$$Indicator of Compromise - an artifact (malicious IP, file hash, domain) suggesting a system has been breached; what threat-detection findings are ultimately built from.$$),
('sc-a-106','awsscs','acronym',1,'1.3',$$SOAR$$,$$Security Orchestration, Automation, and Response - the pattern EventBridge plus Lambda/Step Functions implements natively for AWS-native automated incident remediation.$$),
('sc-a-107','awsscs','acronym',1,'1.3',$$TTP$$,$$Tactics, Techniques, and Procedures - the behavior patterns (e.g., credential exfiltration via SSRF) threat detection and response plans are built around recognizing.$$),
('sc-a-108','awsscs','acronym',1,'1.3',$$MTTR$$,$$Mean Time to Respond - the metric automated, event-driven remediation (versus manual analyst action) is specifically designed to shrink.$$),
-- feynman
('sc-y-050','awsscs','feynman',1,'1.1',$$Explain how AWS Systems Manager Incident Manager's pieces - response plans, contacts, engagements, and runbooks - fit together during a live incident.$$,$$Model answer: A CloudWatch alarm (or a person) triggers a response plan, the pre-built template for this kind of incident. The response plan does three things at once: it starts an engagement, walking through the contacts' escalation plans so the right people get paged in order until someone acknowledges; it opens a chat channel via AWS Chatbot so responders coordinate in one place; and it kicks off the runbook, an SSM Automation document that executes the actual technical response steps. Everything - who was paged, what commands ran, when - lands in the incident's timeline automatically. None of this is improvised: the response plan, the escalation stages, and the runbook were all defined and tested before the incident happened, so the only thing decided in the moment is which response plan fits what's going on.$$),
('sc-y-051','awsscs','feynman',1,'1.1',$$Explain why an incident response plan should pre-provision break-glass access and a forensic IAM role instead of creating them when an incident starts.$$,$$Model answer: Two problems show up if you wait: speed and safety. Creating a least-privilege IAM role correctly takes thought - deciding exactly which actions a forensic investigator needs - and doing that carefully while an attacker is still active wastes precious time, or worse, you cut corners and grant something too broad because you're rushed. There's also an availability problem: if the incident involves your normal SSO or federation path being unavailable or itself compromised, you need a separate way in that doesn't depend on the thing that's broken. So you design the role in advance, scope it tightly, test that it actually works, and store the ability to assume it somewhere resilient. When the real incident hits, responders spend their time investigating, not provisioning.$$),
('sc-y-052','awsscs','feynman',1,'1.2',$$Explain how Security Hub's severity and workflow status fields let a small team triage findings from GuardDuty, Macie, and Inspector without getting overwhelmed.$$,$$Model answer: Without those two fields, every finding from every service would look equally urgent, and an analyst would either re-review the same accepted-risk finding every day or drown in volume. Severity ranks how bad a finding is on its own - a critical GuardDuty credential-exfiltration finding versus an informational Inspector CVE - so the team can sort by what actually matters first. Workflow status tracks where each finding sits in the team's own process: NEW means nobody's looked at it, NOTIFIED means someone's on it, RESOLVED means it's fixed, and SUPPRESSED means it's a known, accepted case that shouldn't keep resurfacing. Combining them, a team can build a view that shows only high/critical findings still in NEW status - the genuinely actionable backlog - instead of re-triaging everything from three different services every single day.$$),
('sc-y-053','awsscs','feynman',1,'1.3',$$Explain why attaching an explicit deny policy revokes a compromised principal's already-active sessions immediately, while just deleting the access key does not.$$,$$Model answer: IAM doesn't check credentials once at login and then trust them forever - every single API call is evaluated against the current policies at the moment it's made. A long-term access key, once deleted, can't be used to sign new requests, but it never protected against temporary credentials that were already issued through AssumeRole - those keep working independently until their own expiration, key deletion or not. An explicit Deny is different: because every call is re-evaluated live, the moment you attach a Deny (especially one scoped with a token-issue-time condition), the very next API call that session tries - even mid-session - gets rejected. So key deletion stops new credential issuance, but only an active Deny stops sessions already in the attacker's hands right now.$$),
('sc-y-054','awsscs','feynman',1,'1.3',$$Explain why isolating a compromised EC2 instance is usually the better first move over terminating it.$$,$$Model answer: The instinct to just kill the bad instance is understandable, but termination is destructive and one-way: the running processes, the contents of memory, active network connections, and anything not written to a persisted volume all disappear the instant it's gone, along with your best chance of understanding what actually happened and how far the attacker got. Isolating it instead - swapping in a quarantine security group that blocks essentially all inbound and outbound traffic - achieves the same immediate goal of stopping further damage and lateral movement, but the instance keeps running. That means you can still snapshot the disk, pull a memory image, and review what's happening on it before you ever decide to shut it down. Containment and evidence preservation aren't in tension if you isolate first; they only conflict if you terminate too early.$$);

insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
-- 1.1 IR plan design
('sc-q-200','awsscs',1,'1.1','ordering',1,$q$Order the four phases of the AWS-recommended security incident response lifecycle, from what happens before any incident occurs through the phase that happens after it's resolved.$q$,$q$["Preparation - tooling, roles, and runbooks are in place","Detection & Analysis - identifying and triaging the event","Containment, Eradication & Recovery - limiting damage, removing the cause, restoring normal operation","Post-Incident Activity - root-cause review and updating the plan"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$The lifecycle starts with Preparation, since none of the later phases work without pre-built tooling, access, and runbooks. Detection & Analysis identifies and scopes an event. Containment, Eradication & Recovery stops the damage, removes the root cause, and restores service. Post-Incident Activity closes the loop with a review that feeds improvements back into Preparation for next time.$q$),
('sc-q-201','awsscs',1,'1.1','mcq',1,$q$What does an Incident Manager response plan actually link together?$q$,$q$["A CloudWatch dashboard and a Route 53 health check","A trigger (alarm or manual start) with an engagement of contacts, a chat channel, and a runbook to execute","An S3 lifecycle rule and a Glacier vault","A VPC and its subnets"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A response plan is the reusable template tying a trigger to who gets engaged, what chat channel opens, and what runbook runs - so the response is defined in advance rather than assembled live. The other options describe unrelated AWS constructs.$q$),
('sc-q-202','awsscs',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$A responder doesn't acknowledge the first page within the configured wait time. What happens under an Incident Manager escalation plan?$q$,$q$["The incident is automatically closed","The plan advances to its next stage, which can notify a different contact or channel","Nothing further happens until someone manually intervenes","GuardDuty is automatically disabled"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Escalation plans are ordered stages, each with its own wait time and notification channel; a missed acknowledgment advances to the next stage, which may page an entirely different person. Incidents don't auto-close from a missed page, and escalation has no effect on GuardDuty.$q$),
('sc-q-203','awsscs',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$How does a runbook differ from a playbook in incident response planning?$q$,$q$["They are the same thing under different names","A runbook is a specific executable procedure for one action; a playbook is the broader decision guide covering which runbooks to use across a whole incident type","A playbook only applies to network incidents","A runbook requires no automation"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A runbook (often an SSM Automation document) executes one well-defined action, like isolating an instance. A playbook is the higher-level guide for an entire incident category, directing which runbooks to invoke, in what order, and who to involve as the situation branches.$q$),
('sc-q-204','awsscs',1,'1.1','mcq',1,$q$Which AWS service lets Incident Manager automatically stand up a dedicated Slack or Chime channel when an incident starts?$q$,$q$["AWS Chatbot","Amazon Connect","AWS Support Center","Amazon Pinpoint"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$AWS Chatbot integrates with Incident Manager to spin up a chat channel automatically as part of the response, centralizing coordination. Connect is a contact-center service, Support Center is for AWS support cases, and Pinpoint is for outbound customer messaging - none create incident chat channels.$q$),
('sc-q-205','awsscs',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$Why should an Incident Manager replication set span at least two AWS Regions?$q$,$q$["It's required for GuardDuty to function","So response plans, contacts, and incident records stay available even if the primary Region has a problem","It reduces Incident Manager's cost to zero","It automatically translates runbooks into every Region's language settings"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The replication set determines which Regions Incident Manager's data is copied to; spanning multiple Regions means you can still manage an incident even if the primary Region is degraded. It has no relationship to GuardDuty, cost, or localization.$q$),
('sc-q-206','awsscs',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$What is a break-glass access pattern designed to solve in incident response planning?$q$,$q$["Reducing the number of IAM roles in an account","Giving responders a pre-provisioned, tested emergency path to access resources when normal access (e.g., SSO) is unavailable or too slow","Automatically fixing security group misconfigurations","Encrypting data at rest by default"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Break-glass access is a deliberately pre-built emergency path - tested in advance with strong logging - for situations where the usual access method is down or too slow, such as during an SSO outage mid-incident. It isn't about reducing role count, auto-remediating security groups, or encryption.$q$),
('sc-q-207','awsscs',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$Why is it best practice to create a forensic IAM role before an incident occurs rather than during one?$q$,$q$["IAM roles can only be created once per account","Creating scoped, least-privilege access under time pressure is slow and error-prone, delaying containment while the attacker remains active","Forensic roles are automatically created by GuardDuty","It has no effect on response speed"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Building a correctly scoped role while actively responding wastes time you don't have and risks over-granting access out of urgency. Pre-provisioning and testing the role means responders can use it the moment an incident is confirmed.$q$),
('sc-q-208','awsscs',1,'1.1','mcq',1,$q$What is the purpose of a tabletop exercise (game day) for an incident response plan?$q$,$q$["To generate revenue from AWS Marketplace","To practice the response plan and runbooks against a simulated scenario and surface gaps before a real incident","To automatically patch EC2 instances","To reduce AWS Support case volume"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A tabletop exercise walks the team through a simulated incident so stale runbook steps, missing contacts, or unclear ownership surface in a low-stakes setting - before they cause delay during a real event.$q$),
('sc-q-209','awsscs',1,'1.1','multi',2,$q$Which TWO of the following belong in a post-incident review (a Correction of Error) after a security incident is resolved?$q$,$q$["A documented root cause and timeline of what happened and what was done","Concrete corrective actions, including updates to the runbooks and response plan","Permanently deleting all CloudTrail logs related to the incident","Reassigning blame to the individual who first noticed the incident"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$A COE should capture what happened and why (root cause and timeline) and turn that into concrete fixes, including updating the very runbooks and plan that were used - closing the loop for next time. Deleting evidence destroys what's needed for review and any later legal or compliance need, and a COE is meant to be blameless, focused on process gaps rather than individuals.$q$),
('sc-q-210','awsscs',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$Why does a mature incident response plan define separate runbooks for a leaked credential, a ransomware infection, and a DDoS attack, instead of one generic runbook?$q$,$q$["AWS requires a minimum number of runbooks per account","Each scenario calls for different first actions and stakeholders, and scenario-specific runbooks let responders act correctly and immediately","Generic runbooks are not supported by SSM Automation","Only one runbook can be attached to a response plan"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Credential compromise, ransomware, and DDoS each demand different immediate steps and different people involved; having the right runbook ready for each means responders execute the correct actions right away instead of adapting a generic checklist under pressure. None of the other statements reflect real AWS constraints.$q$),
('sc-q-211','awsscs',1,'1.1','mcq',2,$q$A company suspects one of its own AWS accounts is being used by an attacker to launch traffic against a third party. According to good IR plan design, what should the pre-documented escalation path include?$q$,$q$["Waiting for the third party to file a complaint before taking action","Contacting AWS Support / AWS Trust & Safety to report the suspected abuse, alongside internal containment steps","Deleting the account immediately with no investigation","Ignoring it since it's the third party's problem"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$AWS provides a channel (via Support/Trust & Safety) for reporting suspected resource abuse, and a well-designed IR plan documents this escalation path and who's authorized to use it in advance, alongside internal containment of the compromised account. Waiting, deleting without investigation, or ignoring it all leave the abuse unresolved and evidence unexamined.$q$),
('sc-q-212','awsscs',1,'1.1','matching',3,$q$Match each Incident Manager concept to what it represents.$q$,$q${"left":["Response plan","Contact / escalation plan","Engagement","Runbook"],"right":["The template tying a trigger to contacts, chat channel, and automation to run","An ordered sequence of notification stages for a specific responder","The active process of paging and involving contacts for a specific incident","The executable SSM Automation document performing the technical response steps"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$The response plan is the overall template; a contact's escalation plan defines how that one person is paged in stages; an engagement is the live act of working through contacts for a specific incident; and the runbook is the automation document that actually performs the technical work. Each concept plays a distinct, complementary role.$q$),
('sc-q-213','awsscs',1,'1.1','mcq',3,$q$A response plan classifies an incident as low severity, and a second finding later raises it to critical mid-incident. What should a well-designed Incident Manager configuration do?$q$,$q$["Nothing - severity is fixed for the life of the incident","Allow updating the incident's severity, which can trigger additional escalation stages and broader notification appropriate to the new impact","Automatically terminate all resources in the account","Require starting an entirely new, separate incident"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Incident Manager lets you update an incident's impact/severity as understanding evolves, and that change can drive further escalation appropriate to the now-higher severity - reflecting reality rather than being locked to the initial assessment. It doesn't force a new incident or take destructive account-wide action automatically.$q$),
-- 1.2 detect
('sc-q-214','awsscs',1,'1.2','multi',2,$q$Which TWO of the following are specialized GuardDuty protection plans that analyze a specific additional data source beyond core threat detection?$q$,$q$["EKS Protection, which analyzes Kubernetes audit logs","RDS Protection, which profiles database login activity for anomalies","IAM Access Analyzer, which flags externally shared resources","AWS Config, which tracks resource configuration drift"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$EKS Protection and RDS Protection are both GuardDuty protection plans that feed an extra data source (Kubernetes audit logs, RDS login activity) into GuardDuty's detection models. IAM Access Analyzer and AWS Config are separate services entirely, not GuardDuty protection plans.$q$),
('sc-q-215','awsscs',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$A company wants GuardDuty automatically enabled for every current and future account in its AWS Organization, with findings visible from one place, without configuring each account individually. What should they do?$q$,$q$["Enable GuardDuty manually in each new account as it's created","Designate a GuardDuty delegated administrator account for the Organization","Attach an SCP that enables GuardDuty","Create a separate GuardDuty detector per Region only"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A GuardDuty delegated administrator centrally manages member accounts, including auto-enabling GuardDuty for new accounts, and aggregates their findings to one account. SCPs restrict actions, they don't enable services, and manual per-account setup is exactly the toil this feature avoids.$q$),
('sc-q-216','awsscs',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$A security team runs authorized penetration-testing scans from a known set of IP addresses and wants GuardDuty to stop generating findings for that traffic, without disabling detection for anything else. What should they configure?$q$,$q$["Disable GuardDuty entirely during the test window","Add the scanner IPs to a GuardDuty trusted IP list","Delete the GuardDuty detector","Add the scanner IPs to a threat list"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A trusted IP list tells GuardDuty a set of IPs is known-safe, suppressing findings for that traffic while every other detection keeps running. Disabling GuardDuty or deleting the detector removes all detection, and a threat list does the opposite - it flags IPs as known-bad.$q$),
('sc-q-217','awsscs',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$What does designating a Security Hub delegated administrator with cross-Region aggregation provide that per-account, per-Region Security Hub does not?$q$,$q$["Lower AWS bill for Security Hub usage","A single account and view that aggregates findings from every enabled member account and Region","Automatic remediation of every finding","Removal of the need for GuardDuty"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Delegated administration plus cross-Region aggregation rolls up findings from many accounts and Regions into one place, instead of an analyst checking each account/Region separately. It doesn't change cost structure, doesn't auto-remediate, and Security Hub still depends on services like GuardDuty as finding sources.$q$),
('sc-q-218','awsscs',1,'1.2','mcq',1,$q$An analyst wants to see only findings that are high or critical severity and haven't yet been triaged by anyone. Which Security Hub finding fields make that filter possible?$q$,$q$["Severity and workflow status","Resource ID and Region","Finding ARN and generator ID","Account ID and creation date"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Severity ranks urgency and workflow status (e.g., NEW versus NOTIFIED/RESOLVED) tracks triage progress - combining both lets an analyst filter to exactly the untriaged, high-priority backlog. The other field pairs identify or locate a finding but don't express urgency or triage state.$q$),
('sc-q-219','awsscs',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$Amazon Inspector reports two findings for the same CVE severity, but flags one instance's finding with a much higher risk score than the other's. What most likely explains the difference?$q$,$q$["Inspector randomly assigns risk scores","The higher-scored instance is network-reachable from the internet, while the other is not","The higher-scored instance has more storage attached","Risk score is unrelated to the finding"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Inspector's risk score factors in exploitability context like network reachability on top of the CVE's own severity - an internet-reachable vulnerable resource scores materially higher than an identical vulnerability on an isolated resource, helping teams prioritize what's actually at risk right now.$q$),
('sc-q-220','awsscs',1,'1.2','matching',3,$q$Match each detection service (or protection plan) to the specific data source or capability that distinguishes it.$q$,$q${"left":["GuardDuty Malware Protection","Amazon Macie","Amazon Inspector","AWS Security Hub"],"right":["Agentless scanning of EBS volumes/S3 objects flagged by a finding, for malware","ML-based discovery and classification of sensitive data in S3","Continuous vulnerability scanning of EC2, ECR images, and Lambda with a risk score","Central aggregation of findings across services with automated compliance-standard checks"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Each service occupies a distinct niche: GuardDuty Malware Protection scans storage for malware without an agent, Macie classifies sensitive data, Inspector finds and scores vulnerabilities, and Security Hub aggregates everyone else's findings into one place with standards checks layered on top.$q$),
('sc-q-221','awsscs',1,'1.2','mcq',3,$q$A security team enables GuardDuty S3 Protection, Macie, and Security Hub together for their S3 data. What is the correct division of labor?$q$,$q$["All three do the exact same thing redundantly","GuardDuty S3 Protection watches for suspicious data-plane access patterns, Macie classifies what sensitive data actually exists, and Security Hub aggregates both sets of findings into one prioritized view","Only Macie is necessary; the other two add nothing","Security Hub replaces the need for GuardDuty and Macie entirely"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$GuardDuty S3 Protection focuses on anomalous access behavior, Macie focuses on classifying the sensitive content itself, and Security Hub doesn't detect anything on its own - it aggregates and prioritizes findings from both (and other sources) into a unified view. None of the three is redundant with the others.$q$),
-- 1.3 respond
('sc-q-222','awsscs',1,'1.3','mcq',3,$q$An IAM role was compromised and used to launch several temporary sessions via AssumeRole before you discovered it. Deleting the role's trust policy won't stop already-issued sessions. What will?$q$,$q$["Deleting the role entirely, which also breaks legitimate workloads","Attaching a policy with an explicit Deny on all actions, conditioned on aws:TokenIssueTime before the compromise was discovered","Waiting for the sessions to expire naturally","Rotating the account's root credentials"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Because IAM evaluates policy on every API call, an explicit Deny scoped with aws:TokenIssueTime immediately blocks any session issued before that time - including ones already in progress - without touching sessions created after the fix. Deleting the role is disruptive and overkill, waiting leaves the attacker active, and root credentials are unrelated to a role's sessions.$q$),
('sc-q-223','awsscs',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$GuardDuty reports UnauthorizedAccess:IAMUser/InstanceCredentialExfiltration.OutsideAWS for an EC2 instance's role. What does this finding indicate?$q$,$q$["The instance's root volume is unencrypted","The instance role's temporary credentials were used to call the AWS API from an IP address outside AWS, suggesting they were stolen and are being used remotely","The instance failed a patch compliance check","The instance is receiving unusually high inbound traffic"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$This finding fires when instance-role credentials, which normally originate calls from within AWS, are observed being used from outside AWS entirely - a strong signal the credentials were exfiltrated (often via SSRF against the instance metadata service) and are now in an attacker's hands. It has nothing to do with encryption, patching, or inbound traffic volume.$q$),
('sc-q-224','awsscs',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$A Security Hub custom action is configured on a set of findings. What triggers the remediation Lambda in this pattern?$q$,$q$["It runs automatically the instant any matching finding is created, with no human involved","An analyst manually selects one or more findings in the console and sends them to the custom action, which triggers an EventBridge rule invoking the Lambda","It runs on a fixed daily schedule regardless of findings","Security Hub calls the Lambda directly with no EventBridge rule involved"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A custom action is a human-in-the-loop trigger: an analyst reviews findings and deliberately sends selected ones to the action, which fires an EventBridge rule that invokes the Lambda. That's distinct from a fully automatic EventBridge rule that matches every finding of a given type with no review step, and EventBridge is the mechanism in between, not a direct Security Hub-to-Lambda call.$q$),
('sc-q-225','awsscs',1,'1.3','mcq',1,$q$During active containment of a suspected compromise, why is isolating an EC2 instance usually preferable to immediately terminating it?$q$,$q$["Isolating is always cheaper than termination","Termination destroys volatile evidence like memory and running processes, while isolation stops further damage while preserving that evidence","Terminated instances can be un-terminated later","Isolation automatically fixes the vulnerability that led to compromise"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Termination is irreversible and wipes out memory contents, running processes, and active connections that investigators need to determine root cause and scope. Isolating with a quarantine security group achieves the same immediate goal - stopping damage - without destroying that evidence. Terminated instances cannot be restored, and isolation doesn't fix the underlying vulnerability by itself.$q$),
('sc-q-226','awsscs',1,'1.3','mcq',1,$q$What should a quarantine security group applied to a compromised instance for containment typically allow?$q$,$q$["All outbound traffic, but no inbound traffic","The same rules as the instance's normal security group, slightly restricted","Essentially nothing - deny all inbound and outbound, with at most a narrow exception for a forensic analysis host","Only traffic to the instance's original Auto Scaling group"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$A quarantine security group should cut off nearly all traffic in both directions to fully sever attacker access and stop lateral movement, with only a tightly scoped exception if a forensic host needs limited access. Allowing broad outbound traffic, reusing the normal rules, or leaving ASG traffic open all leave meaningful compromise paths intact.$q$),
('sc-q-227','awsscs',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$A Lambda function's execution role is suspected of being compromised. Since there's no security group to attach to a function, what's the correct containment approach?$q$,$q$["Nothing can be done to contain a compromised Lambda function","Remove/disable its event source mappings and triggers, and revoke or restrict its execution role's permissions","Increase the function's reserved concurrency","Change the function's runtime version"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Without a network-level isolation mechanism like a security group, containment for Lambda means stopping invocation (removing triggers/event source mappings) and cutting off what the function's role can do (revoking or restricting permissions) - directly addressing both how it gets invoked and what damage it can cause if invoked anyway. Concurrency and runtime settings don't contain a compromise.$q$),
('sc-q-228','awsscs',1,'1.3','mcq',1,$q$Beyond deactivating a compromised IAM user's access keys, what else should be done to fully cut off their sign-in ability?$q$,$q$["Nothing else is needed","Disable or delete the console login profile and any registered MFA devices","Increase the account's password length requirement","Enable S3 Block Public Access"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Deactivating access keys only stops programmatic access; if the user also has a console login profile, the attacker could still sign in through the console unless that login profile (and any MFA device tied to it) is also disabled or removed. Password policy changes and S3 settings don't affect this specific user's existing sign-in ability.$q$),
('sc-q-229','awsscs',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$Why copy a forensic EBS snapshot into a separate, locked-down account before analyzing it, rather than analyzing it in place in the workload account?$q$,$q$["It's required by the EBS API and cannot be skipped","It preserves an untouched original for chain of custody, and moves analysis away from an account that may still be under attacker control","It automatically decrypts the snapshot","It reduces the snapshot's storage cost to zero"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Working in a separate, controlled account keeps the original evidence pristine in case it's needed again (including for legal purposes) and ensures analysis doesn't happen inside an account the attacker might still have a foothold in. It isn't an EBS API requirement, doesn't affect encryption automatically, and doesn't change storage cost.$q$),
('sc-q-230','awsscs',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$An EventBridge rule needs to invoke a pre-built, repeatable set of remediation steps - like isolating an instance and taking a snapshot - rather than inline Lambda code written for one specific incident. What should the target be?$q$,$q$["An SSM Automation document (runbook)","A CloudFormation stack update","A Route 53 health check","A DynamoDB table scan"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$SSM Automation documents encode a repeatable, auditable set of remediation steps that can be invoked as an EventBridge target or a Security Hub custom action - the standard way to make incident response actions consistent and reusable rather than one-off scripts. The other options aren't remediation runbook mechanisms.$q$),
('sc-q-231','awsscs',1,'1.3','ordering',3,$q$A GuardDuty finding indicates a possible compromise of an EC2 instance. Order the response steps from first to last, prioritizing evidence preservation before destructive action.$q$,$q$["Isolate the instance with a quarantine security group to stop further damage","Snapshot the EBS volume and capture memory before anything is altered further","Investigate scope and root cause using the snapshot, CloudTrail, and Detective","Eradicate the cause and terminate or rebuild the instance once the investigation is complete"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$Isolate first to stop the bleeding without destroying anything. Capture evidence next, before further activity (including your own investigation) can alter volatile state. Only then investigate using that preserved evidence to determine full scope and root cause. Eradication and rebuild come last, once you're confident you understand what you're cleaning up - acting before investigating risks missing a persistence mechanism or the true extent of the compromise.$q$),
('sc-q-232','awsscs',1,'1.3','multi',2,$q$Which TWO of the following are appropriate immediate containment actions for an IAM user confirmed to be compromised?$q$,$q$["Deactivate the user's access keys and disable their console login profile","Attach an explicit Deny policy scoped to sessions issued before the compromise","Delete the entire AWS account","Grant the user AdministratorAccess to see what they do next"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Deactivating keys and the login profile stops both programmatic and console access, and a scoped explicit Deny revokes already-issued temporary sessions - both proportionate, immediate containment actions. Deleting the entire account is wildly disproportionate and destroys evidence, and granting more access to a confirmed-compromised identity actively increases risk instead of containing it.$q$),
('sc-q-233','awsscs',1,'1.3','mcq',3,$q$A team wants every GuardDuty finding of a specific high-confidence type to trigger instance isolation within seconds, with zero analyst involvement. Which pattern fits, and why is it different from a Security Hub custom action?$q$,$q$["An EventBridge rule matching that finding type, targeting a Lambda/SSM Automation remediation - it fires automatically on every match with no manual step, unlike a custom action which needs an analyst to select findings first","A Security Hub custom action, because it also runs automatically with no analyst step","A CloudWatch dashboard, since dashboards can trigger remediation directly","An SNS topic alone, since notifications perform remediation"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An EventBridge rule matching the finding type directly, with no custom action in between, is what achieves fully automatic, zero-touch remediation - it fires on every matching event without waiting for a human to select anything. A Security Hub custom action, by contrast, specifically requires an analyst to choose findings in the console first, which is the opposite of zero involvement; dashboards only visualize data and can't trigger actions; and SNS alone only delivers a notification, it doesn't execute remediation.$q$);
